<title>MEXICO: Mexico's Nafin renews commercial paper program.</title>
<headline>Mexico's Nafin renews commercial paper program.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-05-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexican development bank Nacional Financiera (Nafin) said on Tuesday that it had renewed its $315 million commercial paper program in the United States for another year.</p>
<p>The program, structured by Bank of America, was backed by a letter of credit from Societe Generale, which syndicated the loan between a group of 19 commercial banks.</p>
<p>&quot;The program confirms Nacional Financiera's policy of diversifying its base of investors as well as the markets that it has access to,&quot; the development bank said in a statement.  </p>
<p>The syndicating banks were as follows, Nafin said:</p>
<p>- Banca Monte dei Paschi di Siena SPA</p>
<p>- Banco Central Hispanoamerica</p>
<p>- Banco Espanol de Credito</p>
<p>- Bank of America, NT &amp; SA</p>
<p>- Bank of Montreal</p>
<p>- Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi</p>
<p>- Banque Francaise du Commerce Exterior</p>
<p>- Banque Nationale de Paris</p>
<p>- Bayerische Vereinsbank</p>
<p>- Deutsche Bank</p>
<p>- Dresdner Bank</p>
<p>- National Westminster Bank</p>
<p>- Royal Bank of Canada</p>
<p>- Santander</p>
<p>- Societe Generale</p>
<p>- Swiss Bank Corporation</p>
<p>- Tokai Bank</p>
<p>- Toronto-Dominion Bank</p>
<p>- Union Bank of Switzerland</p>
<p>((--Robert Kozak, Mexico City newsroom +525 728 9559))</p>
</text>
